---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../components/Sidebar.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/Carousel.astro';
import LogoWall from '../components/LogoWall.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Ambotics™" description="A new robotics company. Coming soon.">
  <main id="top" class="min-h-screen w-full">
    <div
      class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-6 sm:px-10 lg:px-[84px] pt-16 sm:pt-20 lg:pt-[104px] pb-24"
    >
      <!-- Single grid: sticky left sidebar (logo + nav) + content -->
      <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[340px_minmax(0,1fr)]">
        <Sidebar />

        <!-- Content column -->
        <div class="min-w-0 mt-14 lg:mt-0">
          <h1
            class="font-display ss01 max-w-[360px] text-[clamp(28px,5vw,32px)] font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink"
          >
            A new kind of robotics company.
          </h1>

          <div class="mt-14 lg:mt-20">
            <!-- The four paragraphs are one continuous argument — ¶3 opens "So the
                 question stops being…" — so the section takes a single heading
                 rather than being split at a seam it does not have. -->
            <section id="starting" class="max-w-content">
              <SectionHeading>What changed</SectionHeading>

              <div
                class="mt-5 space-y-10 body-copy text-[14px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.02em]"
              >
                <p>
                  Robots are not new. They have been welding, painting, and lifting in
                  factories since the 1960s, and they have been very good at it. The
                  catch was always the conditions. A conventional robot needs the world
                  held still around it — parts in fixed positions, a cage, and a program
                  written for exactly one task. Change the task and you pay for the
                  integration all over again. That is why robots filled car plants and
                  stayed rare nearly everywhere else.
                </p>
                <p>
                  AI is what changed. A robot can now see what is actually in front of
                  it, handle parts that arrive at odd angles, and pick up a new task by
                  being shown it instead of being reprogrammed. That sounds like a
                  technical detail. It is not. It moves the line of what is worth
                  automating. Small batches, frequent changeovers, and jobs that vary a
                  little every time are now within reach.
                </p>
                <p>
                  So the question stops being whether a machine can physically do the
                  work. It becomes whether doing it that way pays. That is a business
                  question, and most companies in this field are not built to answer it.
                  They lead with the hardware and the breakthrough, and they speak to
                  other engineers. We do the opposite. We lead with your business, in
                  plain language, about your problem. If automating a task does not
                  produce a real return for your operation, we will not take it on. That
                  discipline is not a limitation. It is the whole promise.
                </p>
                <p>
                  We own the entire stack: software, hardware, data, training, and model
                  evaluation, so we can iterate faster than anyone else. When we do take
                  something on, we own all of it. The hardware, the installation, the
                  daily operation, and the support that keeps everything running long
                  after the first day. You will never have to become a robotics expert
                  or assemble a line of vendors to make it work. You show us where the
                  repetitive work lives. We carry the rest.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- The section is deliberately not `max-w-content`: the carousel
                 bleeds to the right page edge with a negative margin, so the
                 width cap goes on the prose and the logo wall individually. -->
            <section id="applications" class="mt-24">
              <SectionHeading>Applications</SectionHeading>

              <div
                class="mt-5 max-w-content space-y-10 body-copy text-[14px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.02em]"
              >
                <p>
                  None of this is theoretical for us. Our team's prior work spans IKEA,
                  ABB, AstraZeneca, and Universal Robots — retail distribution,
                  industrial robotics, and pharmaceutical production. The applications
                  below are the kinds of operation we already know from the inside.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Most of our customers start in the same place. They have a sense that
                  something in their operation could be done better, and they are not
                  sure what is possible or where to begin. That is reasonable. The line
                  moved recently, and it is not obvious from the outside where it now
                  sits.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Tell us where the repetitive work lives. We will tell you plainly
                  whether there is a case for automating it. If there is, we will build
                  it, run it, and stand behind it.
                </p>
              </div>

              <Carousel class="mt-10" />

              <LogoWall class="mt-16" />
            </section>

            <ContactForm />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
